## Current State Analysis

### Data Flow Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["Frontend Widget"] -->|"GET /api/social/:platform/:handle"| B["socialRoutes.js"]
    B -->|"calls"| C["*Service.js\n(Apify Scraper)"]
    C -->|"raw Apify JSON"| B
    B -->|"proxy images + cache"| A
    
    D["blockController.js"] -->|"createBlock / updateBlock"| E["socialProfileService.js"]
    E -->|"calls"| C
    E -->|"normalize + persist"| F["MongoDB\nSocialProfile"]
    D -->|"getUserBlocks / getPublicBlocks\njoins block + socialProfile"| A
```

### Two Parallel Data Paths (Current)

The frontend receives social data from **two separate paths**, and both must be exhaustive:

| Path | Route | Description | Storage |
|------|-------|-------------|---------|
| **Path A** | `GET /api/social/:platform/:handle` | Direct Apify → in-memory cache → JSON response | 30-min in-memory `Map` in `socialRoutes.js` |
| **Path B** | `getOrFetchSocialProfile()` | Apify → normalize → MongoDB `SocialProfile` document | MongoDB (24h TTL) |

**Path A** feeds `socialStats[cacheKey]` in the frontend.
**Path B** feeds `block.socialProfile` when blocks are loaded from MongoDB via `blockController.js`.

> [!CAUTION]
> **Both paths must return the same shape of data.** Currently Path A returns the raw scraper service output (with `recentPosts`, `recentRepos`, `recentVideos`, etc.), while Path B returns the MongoDB `SocialProfile` document (with `recentContent`). The frontend `extractRecentPosts()` helper already searches both shapes, so this works — but the MongoDB path is currently losing data because `extractNormalizedRecentContent` drops fields.

